{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>Rob not the poor, for he is poor, and crush not the afflicted at the gate; for HaShem will plead their cause and despoil the despoilers of them of life</b> (Proverbs 22:22–23)." "<b>Solomon</b> the king, upon him be peace, informed us in these two verses (Proverbs 22) of the punishment of one who robs the poor. Know that there are four classes concerning which the Torah warned man to have compassion upon them and not to do them harm, and they are: the poor, orphans, widows, and converts. And so we find that Scripture warned concerning all four of them in a single verse, as it is written (Deuteronomy 16:14): ״ושמחת בחגך אתה ובנך ובתך ועבדך ואמתך והלוי והגר והיתום והאלמנה״, and our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'four of Mine against four of yours — if you gladden Mine, I shall gladden yours.' And He mentioned the Levite corresponding to the poor, for Levites are poor, since they had no portion in the land; rather, they would draw their sustenance from the High Table and benefit from the consecrated gifts of Heaven, and they are the army of the Holy One, blessed be He, as it is written (Deuteronomy 33:11): ״ברך ה׳ חילו״. And the Holy One, blessed be He, is their portion, as it is said (Numbers 18:20): ״אני חלקך ונחלך״. The poor — concerning them the Torah warned with a positive commandment and a negative commandment: with a positive commandment, as it is written (Deuteronomy 15:8): ״נתון תתן״. And it is written (ibid. 8): ״פתוח תפתח את ידך לו״. A negative commandment, as it is written (ibid. 10): ״ולא ירע לבבך בתתך לו״, and it is written (ibid. 7): ״ולא תקפוץ את ידך״. And since the Torah already warned concerning robbery in the general prohibition of ״לא תגזול״ for the entire world, it had no need to warn specifically concerning robbery of the poor — for he is obligated to give to him, so how could he rob from him? Therefore Solomon, peace be upon him, came and added an explanation of the punishment for one who robs the poor. For it is not his way throughout the entire book of Proverbs to mention any commandment from those written in the Torah unless he adds an explanation or introduces something new concerning it, for what man would come after Moses if not to make a fence around his words? Therefore he said: ״אל תגזול דל כי דל הוא״ — and one need not say 'the poor,' for it is forbidden to rob even a rich man; and the reason he mentioned the poor is because it is common that all rob him, since he has none to help him and none to speak on his behalf as a rich man has. Rather, everyone hates him and distances themselves from him, even his relatives, as it is written (Proverbs 19:7): ״כל אחי רש שנאוהו אף כי מרעהו רחקו ממנו״. Therefore he says: rob not the poor — from the very fact that he is poor and lacks strength, and you know that he has none to help or support him against you and none to plead his cause — do not imagine this in your soul, for the Holy One, blessed be He, will plead his cause. He is his advocate, and He will punish those who rob him, not with a monetary punishment but with a punishment of body and soul; this is ״וקבע את קובעיהם נפש״ — a word that embraces both body and soul. Orphans and widows — concerning them the Torah warned in many places (Exodus 22:21): ״כל אלמנה ויתום לא תענון״. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: even the widow of a king, and even the orphans of a king. The reason is that their spirit is lowly and their tears are ever-present, and it is a common thing that everyone harms and afflicts them. And Scripture says (ibid.): ״אם ענה תענה אותו״ — this refers back to each one of them, to the orphan and to the widow mentioned. And it doubled the language of affliction because of the persistence of people in afflicting them, and just as it doubled the affliction, so it doubled the outcry and also the hearing of HaShem, may He be exalted; this is (ibid.): ״כי אם צעוק יצעק אלי שמוע אשמע צעקתו״. And since other afflicted people will trouble themselves to seek deliverers and place their trust in them, but the orphan and the widow, who have no deliverers, trust only in the Holy One, blessed be He, therefore the Holy One, blessed be He, hears their outcry and pleads their cause. And so Solomon, peace be upon him, spoke concerning the orphans in this very chapter, saying: ״אל תסג גבול עולם ובשדי יתומים אל תבא, כי גואלם חזק הוא יריב את ריבם אתך״. Converts — concerning them the Torah warned in many places, as it is written (Exodus 22:20): ״וגר לא תונה ולא תלחצנו״, etc. He warns: do not afflict him with verbal oppression, and do not oppress him with robbery of money, ״כי גרים הייתם בארץ מצרים״. And Scripture added another reason: ״ואתם ידעתם את נפש הגר״ — it did not say את הגר but את נפש הגר, meaning: his soul is lowly and submissive, and his eyes are always turned toward HaShem. And so our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: if he is the son of converts, do not say to him, 'Remember the deeds of your ancestors'; if he comes to learn Torah, do not say to him, 'This mouth that ate nevelot and terefot, creeping and crawling things — shall it come and learn Torah that was given from the mouth of the Almighty?' — ״כי גרים הייתם בארץ מצרים״: the blemish that is in you, do not say to your fellow. And so we find that the righteous are called גרים (sojourners). And the word גר is from the root of גרגיר — a grain separated from its source; and so the righteous person sees himself as solitary, his dwelling not in this earth but only a temporary dwelling. And this is what David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119:19): ״גר אנכי בארץ אל תסתר ממני מצותיך״. He likened himself to a גר who is prepared for a journey and does not know the hour; and since he does not know the hour, he must take provisions for the road lest the hour come upon him suddenly — and what are the provisions? The fulfillment of the commandments; this is ״אל תסתר ממני מצותיך״. And so we find that all the patriarchs were called גרים. Abraham — as it is written (Genesis 23:4): ״גר ותושב אנכי עמכם״. Isaac — as it is written (Genesis 26:3): ״גור בארץ הזאת״. Jacob — as it is written:" "<b>And Jacob dwelt in the land of his father's sojournings, in the land of Canaan.</b> Scripture says וישב, 'dwelt,' and did not say ויגר יעקב, 'Jacob sojourned,' because it mentioned above concerning Esau: ״וישב עשו בהר שעיר״. For the descendants of Esau — the kings and the chieftains who issued from him — settled in the land of their possession, the land that had come to them as an everlasting inheritance; therefore it states here concerning Jacob, that he settled in the Holy Land, in the land of his father's sojournings. And it used the expression מגורי because we find that Scripture called it thus (Exodus 6:4): ״לתת להם את ארץ מגוריהם אשר גרו בה״, for since the patriarchs' origin was from beyond the River, they were now גרים in a land that at that time belonged to Canaan." "<b>By way of the Midrash.</b> ״וישב יעקב״ — everywhere the word וישב is said, it is nothing but an expression of distress: (Exodus 32:6) ״וישב העם לאכול ושתו ויקומו לצחק״; (Genesis 37:25) ״וישבו לאכול לחם וישאו עינים ויראו״, etc.; (I Kings 5) ״וישב יהודה וישראל לבטח״, and there arose a satan against Israel; (Numbers 25:1) ״וישב ישראל בשטים ויחל העם״, etc. Here too: וישב — he sought to dwell in tranquility, and the wrath of Joseph burst upon him. The Holy One, blessed be He, said: Is it not enough for the righteous that what is prepared for them in the World to Come suffices them, that they also seek to dwell in tranquility in this world? What is written above? ״ואלה המלכים אשר מלכו בארץ אדום״, ״ואלה שמות אלופי עשו״ — Jacob saw the chieftains of Esau and was afraid. He said: Who can stand against all these kings and chieftains? To what may the matter be compared? To camels loaded with straw — the goldsmith was standing and marveling: where will all that straw enter? A certain wise man was there and said to him: Why do you marvel? A single spark that issues from your crucible will consume all the straw. So too, when Jacob saw Esau and his chieftains and his warriors, he was afraid. The Holy One, blessed be He, said to him: A single spark of yours will consume them — as it is written (Obadiah 1:18): ״והיה בית יעקב אש ובית יוסף להבה ובית עשו לקש ודלקו בהם ואכלום״. And this is what is written: ״אלה תולדות יעקב יוסף״ — that is to say, a spark issues from the progeny of Jacob, namely Joseph, and he will consume all the progeny of Esau, which Jacob feared."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 37, :register {:flags {1 ["the Almighty"]}}, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:18:33.930585143Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}